        "id": "ch5_3_1-3",
        "text": "this allows us to put multiple statements on one line instead of splitting it over multiple lines. This is\neasier to type but not easier to read. Let\u2019s go through the command;\n\u2022 Our first statement is $l = 0 ; . This creates the $l variable and sets it to 0 . This means\neach time we run the whole line, it will reset $l to 0 .\n\u2022 The second statement is Foreach ($i in Get-ChildItem *.ps1) {$l +=\n$i.length} ; . This is where we create our array and loop through each element of it.\n\u2022I Our final statement is just $l \u2013 this returns the value of $l at the end of the loop.\nPowerShell Control Flow \u2013 Conditionals and Loops\n128\nWe can see how this looks in the following screenshot:\nFigure 5.14 \u2013 The foreach loop and statement separator\nWe\u2019ll be using foreach loops quite a lot, as they are the most common type of loop. Let\u2019s move on\nto the next types, do while and do until .",
